# StrictDoc Requirements in Commit Messages

## Requirement

Once StrictDoc requirements are laid down, linkability in the source SHALL stay, and the commit message SHALL name the same requirements.

Code and tests SHALL keep the requirement UID in a comment:

```c
/* Implements: SSS012 */
```

```c
/* Verifies: SSS012 */
```

The commit that completes the work SHALL also carry those requirements, in addition to the git attribution template (prompt, context, justification, model, token estimate, and the other fields in `git/commit-format.mdc`).

Because each commit is already atomic (`git/commit-atomicity.mdc`), the message is enough to see which requirements that commit completed. A reader SHALL be able to follow them from `git log` alone.

The paired rule is `git/commit-requirement-ids.mdc`. This file and that file are one requirement. A change to either SHALL be reflected in the other.

## What to Name

After the change list and before `-----`:

```
Requirements:
- Specifies SSS012: The software shall open the contactor within 100 ms when DC bus voltage exceeds the trip threshold.
- Implements SSS012: The software shall open the contactor within 100 ms when DC bus voltage exceeds the trip threshold.
```

Each line SHALL be `- <role> <UID>: <one-line statement>`.

Roles:

- `Specifies` — this commit adds or changes that requirement in an `.sdoc` file
- `Implements` — this commit's code is what satisfies that requirement
- `Verifies` — this commit's test is what checks that requirement

The UID SHALL be the requirement `UID` as written in the `.sdoc` file (for example `SSS012`). SHALL NOT invent a UID. When strictdoc-mcp is available, SHALL use the HRID it returns (`strictdoc/mcp-integration.mdc`).

The statement SHALL be that requirement's `STATEMENT`, shortened to one line without changing its meaning.

List only requirements this commit specifies, implements, or verifies. When an atomic commit updates the requirement and its implementation together (Exception 2 in `git/commit-atomicity.mdc`), list each role on its own line.

A commit that completes none of them SHALL use exactly:

```
Requirements:
- None
```

Before any `.sdoc` requirement exists, omit the section.

## Check

`git log --grep=SSS012` SHALL show the commits that completed SSS012, and each of those messages SHALL include the one-line statement.
